Here is the JavaScript code to sort an array of objects by a numeric field called `count`.

The `sort()` method accepts a comparator function. When sorting numbers, you must return the result of the subtraction (`a - b`) to ensure ascending order.

```javascript
// 1. Define the array of objects with the provided counts
const items = [
  { name: "Item A", count: 13 },
  { name: "Item B", count: 26 },
  { name: "Item C", count: 20 }
];

// 2. Sort the array in ascending order based on the 'count' field
items.sort((a, b) => a.count - b.count);

// 3. Output the result to verify
console.log("Sorted Array:");
console.log(items);

// Optional: Just print the counts to see the order clearly
console.log("Counts in order:", items.map(item => item.count));
```

### Output Explanation
When you run this code, the array will be reordered so that the objects appear based on their `count` from lowest to highest:

1.  **13** (Item A)
2.  **20** (Item C)
3.  **26** (Item B)

**Final Sorted Array:**
```javascript
[
  { name: "Item A", count: 13 },
  { name: "Item C", count: 20 },
  { name: "Item B", count: 26 }
]
```

*Note: If you need **descending** order (highest to lowest), simply change the subtraction to `b.count - a.count`.*
